    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Muslim dynasty of Arab origin that ruled Shirvan, in the north-east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, from 861 to 1538.</li><li>It survived by accepting the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> in turn.</li><li>Its court patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaqani">Khaqani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalilullah-i">Khalilullah I</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shirvanshahs">Palace of the Shirvanshahs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a>, one of the most retold love stories of the Islamic world, was composed in 1188 at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i">Shirvanshah Akhsitan I</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

