    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="Harun al-Rashid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Harun_al-Rashid,_AH_170-193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8/9th-century master(s), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shirvan, the land between the lower Kura river, the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, roughly the north-east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, had been a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, governed from the frontier province of Arminiya. Its title, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a>, 'king of Shirvan', was an old Persian one. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> the region was governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-ibn-mazyad-al-shaybani">Yazid ibn Mazyad al-Shaybani</a>, an Arab commander whose family, the Yazidids or Mazyadids, put down roots there. In 861, as caliphal authority collapsed after the murder of al-Mutawakkil, his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haytham-ibn-khalid">Haytham ibn Khalid</a> declared himself Shirvanshah and made the governorship a hereditary principality. The dynasty ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, also called Yazidiyya after the family, and gradually extended its authority over neighbouring districts, absorbing Layzan in the early tenth century and at times controlling the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> to the north. The Yazidids began as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> but became thoroughly Persianised: from the early eleventh century the rulers bore Persian names such as Manuchihr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fariburz-i">Fariburz</a> and claimed descent from the Sasanian kings, and modern historians often call this later line the Kasranids, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisra-borgu-tradition">Kisra</a>, the Arabic form of Khosrow. A collateral branch, the Derbendi line, took power in 1382 and ruled until the end of the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shamakhi.jpg" alt="Shamakhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montage_of_%C5%9Eamax%C4%B1_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sefer azeri, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shirvan was a fertile and productive land. Its foothills and valleys produced grain, fruit and wine, and its mulberry groves supported a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry whose output made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> one of the great silk markets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and would later draw merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absheron-peninsula">Absheron</a> peninsula was known for its oil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and naphtha, which medieval geographers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">al-Mas'udi</a> described, and for salt, while its harbour linked Shirvan to the trade of the Caspian with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">Caravan routes</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> and the steppe passed through the country. The population was mixed, including Iranian-speaking Tats, Caucasian peoples of the mountains, Arab settlers and, from the eleventh century, growing numbers of Turkic tribes, whose language slowly spread across the plains. The court was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim and Persian in culture, and the twelfth century brought a brilliant literary flowering: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaqani">Khaqani Shirvani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falaki-shirvani">Falaki Shirvani</a> wrote at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihr-iii">Manuchihr III</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a> composed <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a></em> in 1188 at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i">Shirvanshah Akhsitan I</a>. Shamakhi suffered repeated earthquakes, and according to tradition the court moved to Baku for a time after a severe one in 1191–1192, though Shamakhi long remained a royal seat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia.jpg" alt="David IV of Georgia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV of Georgia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_IV,_detail_from_icon,_St._Catherine_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Certain types of Egyptian works (official documents, stamps, works of national folklore) should use specific license tags instead …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshahs</a> commanded modest forces, drawn from their own retinue, the local nobility and mountain levies, and they relied on fortresses and diplomacy more than on large armies. The country was protected by fortified towns and castles such as Gulistan above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, Shabaran near the Caspian coast, and the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, but it lay on the main route between the steppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and was repeatedly invaded. Rus raiders struck the Caspian coast in the tenth century, and in the eleventh the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> and Turkic tribes pressed into Shirvan. In the early 1120s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV the Builder</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> campaigned in the country, and for a century Shirvan was drawn into the Georgian sphere. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> took Shamakhi in 1221, and from the 1230s Shirvan was subject to Mongol rule. In the 1260s Shirvan became a battleground between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, whose armies fought repeatedly along the Kura and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek</a>. Ibrahim I submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1386 and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid</a> support, held off the Golden Horde, but in 1412 he and his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> of Georgia were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> of the Qara Qoyunlu, and Ibrahim was captured and later ransomed. In 1460 and 1488 Shirvanshah forces killed the Safavid shaykhs Junayd and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Haydar</a>, a feud that would destroy the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baku"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baku.jpg" alt="Baku" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baku_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See above. Compilation by Interfase, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> state was a hereditary monarchy that survived by accepting the overlordship of whichever great power dominated the region while keeping control of its own affairs. Its rulers were tributaries in turn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>, paying tribute and supplying troops while the dynasty endured at home. Marriage alliances supported this policy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihr-iii">Manuchihr III</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tamar">Tamar</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV of Georgia</a>, and Georgian influence was strong at his court. The rulers governed through a court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and a network of local lords, and the dynasty's long rule rested on its legitimacy among the local nobility, its control of trade revenues and the protection of fortresses. The Derbendi line restored strong government after 1382. Ibrahim I, a member of a collateral branch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, was chosen by the nobles after the old line ended, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalilullah-i">Khalilullah I</a>, who ruled for over four decades from 1417, gave Shirvan its longest period of peace and prosperity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shirvanshahs">Palace of the Shirvanshahs</a> in Baku, with its divankhana, mausoleum of 1435 and mosque of 1441, was the administrative and ceremonial centre of this revived state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shirvanshahs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shirvanshahs.jpg" alt="Palace of the Shirvanshahs" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shirvanshahs">Palace of the Shirvanshahs</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_Shirvanshahs_common.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Urek Meniashvili, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For nearly seven centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshahs</a> preserved a separate Muslim state on the north-western edge of the Iranian world, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, the steppe and the Caspian. Their court was one of the important centres of Persian literature in the twelfth century: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaqani">Khaqani</a>, whose prison poems and ode on the ruins of the Sasanian palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> are classics of Persian verse, served the Shirvanshahs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a></em>, written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i">Akhsitan I</a>, became one of the most influential romances of the Islamic world, retold by poets in Persian, Turkic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> for centuries. The dynasty's buildings, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shirvanshahs">Palace of the Shirvanshahs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, are among the finest monuments of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and since 2000 the palace has formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/icherisheher">Walled City of Baku</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maiden-tower">Maiden Tower</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> of Shirvan and the oil of Baku, both developed under the dynasty, remained central to the economy of the region in later centuries. The Shirvanshahs' feud with the Safavid order, in which two Safavid leaders died, also shaped the founding legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Iran</a>. In modern Azerbaijan the Shirvanshahs are remembered as a native dynasty and their state as an important chapter of national history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jabani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-jabani.jpg" alt="Battle of Jabani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jabani">Battle of Jabani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1541-Battle_in_the_war_between_Shah_Isma%27il_and_the_King_of_Shirvan-Shahnama-i-Isma%27il.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> state was bound up with the rise of the Safavid order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>. In 1460 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd">Shaykh Junayd</a>, the order's militant leader, was killed in battle with the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalilullah-i">Khalilullah I</a>, and in 1488 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a> was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukh-yasar">Farrukh Yasar</a>, who was aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>. Haydar's young son Ismail survived, and when he began his rise to power in 1500 his first major campaign was against Shirvan. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jabani">Battle of Jabani</a> in 1500 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> army defeated and killed Farrukh Yasar, and Ismail then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. The dynasty survived under Safavid supremacy, as Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Iran</a> was occupied with his wider conquests, and rulers such as Ibrahim II Shaykhshah and Khalilullah II governed as tributary princes. After Khalilullah II's death, in 1538 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a> sent an army under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alqas-mirza">Alqas Mirza</a>, which overcame the last claimants and annexed Shirvan as a province of the Safavid Empire, with Alqas as its governor. Members of the dynasty led revolts in the following decades, sometimes with Ottoman support, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> occupied Shirvan in 1578, but the Shirvanshah state was never restored.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> State ruled the north-east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> for nearly seven centuries by balancing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaqani">Khaqani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>, built the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, and fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Iran</a> after killing two of its founding shaykhs.</em></p>

